{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428947,"nonce":"AhbsQKuv/cCekYvT1owv0Mhhe3E=","message":"Fixed — both clauses collapsed to one implementation, and the pair turned out to have ALREADY DIVERGED.\n\n§C.7: notification/domain.IdempotencyKey is now a three-line adapter over alerts/domain.ComputeIdempotencyKey; notify.go untouched. The adapter is not redundancy — SubjectKind and Reason are notification's closed enums and the kernel may import no other domain package (§C.9), so the types stop at the adapter and the bytes are the kernel's. Byte-identical for every input.\n\n§C.6: the raw-map constraint was addressed, not assumed away. New alerts/domain.CanonMap(map[string]string) []byte IS Labels.Canonical reached without the constructor, so §C.1 still has exactly one implementation — I explicitly avoided fixing C.6 duplication by introducing C.1 duplication. It returns []byte, never a lenient Labels, because an unchecked Labels constructor would be a hole through validation layer 3. rules/domain.Fingerprint and Canon now delegate and snapshot.go lost ~80 lines.\n\nTHE FINDING: the kernel took time.Duration and wrote int64(d/time.Second); the live path took float seconds and wrote FormatFloat. So `for: 1s500ms` had TWO different content addresses — '1' vs '1.5'. TestFingerprintAgreesWithTheKernel could not see it because its corpus was whole seconds, the only inputs both spellings could express. A cross-check over the intersection of two domains proves agreement only on the intersection. No stored value changes: every rule_fingerprint in the DB came through rules/domain.Fingerprint, and the kernel had no production caller, so the truncating spelling was a latent re-keying bug rather than a design choice.\n\nlintreach widened from New* to New*+Compute*. It immediately found the same trap a THIRD time — ComputeSourceFingerprint, with production calling LabelSet.Fingerprint() directly in three places. Baselined with a reason rather than deleted, because removing it means editing SPEC §C.3.","files":null}]}